(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-physiology-master-exam-version-5"]={"title":"Physiology Master Exam — Comprehensive (70 Questions)","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"Red blood cells contain abundant carbonic anhydrase. How does this enzyme support the blood's role in gas transport?","choices":["It speeds hydration of CO2 to carbonic acid, letting most CO2 travel as bicarbonate","It loads oxygen onto the heme groups so that each red cell carries more O2 per pass","It binds free iron and keeps hemoglobin locked in its oxygen-carrying ferrous state","It buffers lactic acid from the red cell's own anaerobic glucose metabolism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: carbonic anhydrase catalyzes CO2 + H2O to H2CO3, so most CO2 is carried as HCO3-, the major extracellular buffer. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 3"},{"q":"A hematocrit of 42% tells you which of the following about a whole-blood sample?","choices":["Red cells occupy about 42% of whole-blood volume, the remainder being plasma","Plasma proteins account for close to 42% of the blood's osmotic pressure","About 42% of circulating hemoglobin is currently saturated with oxygen","Hemoglobin makes up roughly 42% of the mass of each individual red cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: hematocrit is the packed cell (mostly RBC) volume as a percent of whole blood, normally ~40-45%. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 6"},{"q":"Each hemoglobin molecule can transport four O2 molecules. Which structural feature accounts for this?","choices":["Four heme groups, each with one iron atom that reversibly binds one O2","Four globin chains that each dissolve one O2 into the red-cell cytoplasm","Four carbonic anhydrase sites that each convert one O2 for transport","Four disulfide bonds that each trap a single dissolved oxygen molecule"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: adult hemoglobin has four globin chains, each with a heme/iron that reversibly binds one O2, so four O2 per molecule. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 21"},{"q":"Chronic hypoxia raises red-cell production largely through which mechanism?","choices":["Sympathetic signaling to the spleen that releases stored mature red cells","Direct hypoxic stimulation of the marrow stem cells acting independently of any hormone","Stabilized HIF-1 drives kidney erythropoietin output, boosting marrow erythropoiesis","Increased hepatic transferrin release that accelerates hemoglobin assembly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: hypoxia stabilizes HIF-1, which increases renal erythropoietin, stimulating marrow production of red cells. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 14-16"},{"q":"Deficiency of vitamin B12 or folate produces abnormally large red cells because these vitamins are required for...","choices":["DNA synthesis, so nuclear maturation lags while the cytoplasm keeps growing","hemoglobin assembly, so cells swell to hold the excess unbound globin","iron incorporation, so cells enlarge to compensate for low oxygen content","membrane repair, so cells round up and expand as they lose deformability"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: without B12/folate, thymidine and thus DNA cannot be made, so the cell grows without dividing, forming large macrocytes. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 17"},{"q":"Von Willebrand factor contributes to primary hemostasis mainly by...","choices":["converting prothrombin to thrombin on the surface of activated platelets","bridging platelets to exposed collagen so they adhere at the injury site","cleaving fibrinogen into the fibrin strands that anchor the platelet mass","triggering vascular smooth muscle spasm to narrow the damaged vessel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: von Willebrand factor binds platelets to exposed subendothelial collagen, promoting adhesion of the plug. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 34"},{"q":"Which event is considered the rate-limiting step of blood coagulation?","choices":["Cleavage of fibrinogen by thrombin into circulating fibrin monomers","Formation of prothrombin activator, which then converts prothrombin to thrombin","Aggregation of platelets into the initial soft plug that forms over the wounded vessel","Cross-linking of fibrin strands by activated fibrin-stabilizing factor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: generating prothrombin activator is the rate-limiting step; it converts prothrombin to thrombin in the presence of calcium. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 40"},{"q":"The extrinsic pathway is triggered when tissue factor is exposed. The extrinsic and intrinsic pathways then converge by...","choices":["activating factor X, which assembles prothrombin activator","activating factor XII on contact with subendothelial collagen","cleaving fibrinogen directly without first forming thrombin","releasing thromboxane A2 to recruit additional nearby platelets"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: both pathways activate factor X, forming prothrombin activator in the common pathway. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 44-47"},{"q":"On healthy, intact endothelium, what normally limits inappropriate clot formation?","choices":["Rapid platelet turnover that clears activated factors before they can act","Continuous low-level plasmin release that digests any fibrin as it forms","High local calcium that keeps the clotting factors in their inactive states","A smooth glycocalyx plus thrombomodulin that diverts thrombin toward protein C"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the smooth glycocalyx repels platelets/factors, and thrombomodulin binds thrombin to activate protein C, which inactivates factors Va and VIIIa. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 48"},{"q":"Vitamin K is required for the liver to make functional prothrombin and factors VII, IX, and X because it...","choices":["transports the finished factors from hepatocytes into the circulating plasma","supplies the phospholipid surface where these clotting factors are assembled","enables carboxylation of glutamate residues needed for calcium binding","cleaves the inactive factor precursors into their active protease forms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: vitamin K is the cofactor for carboxylating glutamate residues, allowing these factors to bind calcium and function. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 54"},{"q":"During acute infection, why do neutrophils dominate the earliest response while macrophages dominate later?","choices":["Neutrophils recirculate for weeks, so they reach tissue before slower monocytes","Macrophages act only after antibodies have coated the pathogen for ingestion","Macrophages must first divide within the tissue before they can phagocytize","Neutrophils are mature and arrive within an hour; macrophages take days to build up"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: mature neutrophils respond immediately, whereas monocytes must accumulate and mature into macrophages over days. — Blood & Immunology, slides 28-30"},{"q":"Kupffer cells lining the liver sinusoids illustrate which principle of the monocyte-macrophage system?","choices":["Neutrophils mature in the liver before entering the general circulation","Lymphocytes convert into macrophages once they reach the portal vein","Circulating platelets become tissue phagocytes at sites of slow blood flow","Monocytes settle in tissue as fixed macrophages that filter passing blood"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: fixed tissue macrophages such as Kupffer cells strip bacteria from portal blood, filtering the circulation. — Blood & Immunology, slides 20-23"},{"q":"Opsonization increases phagocytosis of a bacterium by...","choices":["attracting neutrophils along a chemical gradient toward the microbe","coating it with antibody and complement C3b that phagocytes recognize","fusing lysosomes with the phagosome to form a digestive vesicle","digesting its protective capsule so the cell surface becomes rough"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: opsonins such as antibody and C3b bind the microbe and mark it for recognition and ingestion by phagocytes. — Blood & Immunology, slides 15-16"},{"q":"The complement system enhances immunity primarily by...","choices":["producing the memory cells responsible for a faster secondary response","presenting antigen fragments to helper T-cells on MHC molecules","generating antigen-specific antibodies tailored to each pathogen","opsonizing microbes, driving inflammation, and lysing target membranes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: complement complements antibody and phagocyte action through opsonization, inflammation, and membrane lysis. — Blood & Immunology, slide 53"},{"q":"Which describes a direct way antibodies neutralize a bacterial toxin?","choices":["Inserting pore-forming perforins directly into the toxin molecule","Binding and covering the toxic site so it cannot act on host cells","Secreting interleukins that suppress further toxin production","Phagocytizing the toxin and digesting it within a phagolysosome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: neutralization occurs when antibodies cover the toxic/active site of the antigen so it cannot bind host tissue. — Blood & Immunology, slide 52"},{"q":"Why can a cytotoxic T-cell not respond to free, intact antigen the way a B-cell can?","choices":["T-cells respond only to polysaccharide antigens, never to proteins","T-cells recognize only antigen fragments displayed on MHC molecules","T-cells lack surface receptors and rely entirely on secreted antibody","T-cells must first be opsonized by complement before they can bind antigen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: unlike B-cells, T-cells recognize processed antigen fragments presented by MHC, not free intact antigen. — Blood & Immunology, slide 55"},{"q":"Which pairing correctly matches a defense with its immune category?","choices":["Antigen-specific plasma cells — innate; lysozyme in tears — acquired","Complement proteins — acquired; helper T-cell clones — innate immunity","Gastric acid and intact skin — innate; circulating antibodies — acquired","Memory B-cells — innate; natural killer cells — acquired immunity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: skin and gastric acid are nonspecific innate barriers, while antigen-specific antibodies are acquired immunity. — Blood & Immunology, slides 35-37"},{"q":"Humoral and cell-mediated immunity differ in that humoral immunity...","choices":["provides no memory, while cell-mediated generates lasting protection","acts only inside infected cells, while cell-mediated acts in the blood","requires no prior antigen exposure, while cell-mediated must be triggered","uses B-cell-derived antibodies, while cell-mediated uses activated T-cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: humoral immunity works through B-cell antibodies; cell-mediated immunity works through activated T-lymphocytes. — Blood & Immunology, slide 37"},{"q":"Why is the secondary response to a repeated antigen faster and stronger than the first?","choices":["Complement proteins are consumed the first time and later regenerate larger","The innate system is permanently reprogrammed to recognize that antigen","Plasma cells from the first exposure never die and keep secreting antibody","Memory B- and T-cells from the first exposure persist and react quickly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: memory lymphocytes generated on first exposure remain and mount a rapid, amplified secondary response. — Blood & Immunology, slide 48"},{"q":"In an immediate (type I) allergic reaction, symptoms arise when...","choices":["antigen cross-links IgE bound to mast cells, triggering histamine release","cytotoxic cells lyse host cells that display the offending antigen","antibodies precipitate with soluble antigen and deposit in tissues","T-cells slowly infiltrate the skin over one to two days after contact"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: in immediate hypersensitivity, antigen cross-links IgE on mast cells/basophils, causing degranulation and histamine release. — Blood & Immunology, slides 64-65"},{"q":"The peristaltic reflex ('law of the gut') moves contents aborally by...","choices":["contracting the entire intestine at once to squeeze contents forward","relying on sympathetic input to drive rhythmic forward propulsion","contracting the segment behind the bolus while relaxing the segment ahead","alternating haustral pouches that slowly knead contents toward the anus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: distension causes contraction orad (behind) with receptive relaxation downstream, propelling contents forward. — GI Physiology, slide 39"},{"q":"Which hormone-action pairing is correct for the digestive response to a meal?","choices":["CCK — is the principal stimulus for gastric acid from parietal cells","Secretin — stimulates pancreatic bicarbonate to neutralize duodenal acid","Gastrin — relaxes the gallbladder and constricts the sphincter of Oddi","Motilin — is released after meals to speed nutrient absorption"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: secretin, released by duodenal acid, stimulates pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate, acting as 'nature's antacid'. — GI Physiology, slides 14-16"},{"q":"How does gastrin most powerfully stimulate gastric acid secretion?","choices":["Largely by driving ECL cells to release histamine, which acts on parietal cells","By converting pepsinogen into active pepsin within the acidic gastric lumen itself","By stimulating duodenal S-cells to release secretin into the circulating bloodstream","By directly neutralizing somatostatin's inhibition of the chief cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: much of gastrin's potent acid effect is indirect, through histamine released from ECL cells acting on parietal cells. — GI Physiology, slides 88-91"},{"q":"Late in a meal, as gastric contents become very acidic, acid secretion declines because...","choices":["histamine is depleted faster than ECL cells can regenerate it","secretin from the stomach directly stimulates further gastrin output","parietal cells run out of the carbonic anhydrase needed to make acid","low pH triggers somatostatin, which suppresses gastrin release"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: falling pH stimulates somatostatin and directly inhibits gastrin, providing negative feedback that reduces acid output. — GI Physiology, slides 90, 94-95"},{"q":"The pancreas avoids digesting itself largely because...","choices":["pancreatic tissue simply lacks any receptors for its own digestive enzymes entirely","proteases are stored and secreted as inactive zymogens activated only in the gut","bicarbonate keeps the acinar pH too high for any enzyme to function","enzymes are secreted so slowly that ongoing tissue repair always manages to keep pace"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: enzymes like trypsinogen are stored inactive (with a trypsin inhibitor) and activated only in the intestine. — GI Physiology, slides 103-105"},{"q":"Enterokinase (enteropeptidase) on the intestinal brush border initiates protein digestion by...","choices":["emulsifying protein aggregates so that pepsin can reach them","converting trypsinogen to trypsin, which then activates the other proteases","activating pancreatic amylase so that it can begin breaking down starch","cleaving dietary protein directly into individual absorbable free amino acids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: enterokinase converts trypsinogen to trypsin, which autocatalytically activates chymotrypsinogen and procarboxypeptidase. — GI Physiology, slide 104"},{"q":"Bile salts aid fat absorption because their amphipathic structure lets them...","choices":["convert dietary cholesterol into water-soluble bile pigments for excretion","chemically hydrolyze triglycerides into fatty acids and glycerol","emulsify fat and ferry lipid digestion products to the mucosa as micelles","actively pump fatty acids across the enterocyte membrane using ATP"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: being amphipathic, bile salts emulsify fat and form micelles that carry monoglycerides and fatty acids to the mucosa. — GI Physiology, slide 20"},{"q":"Glucose and fructose are absorbed by the enterocyte through different routes because...","choices":["glucose diffuses freely through the membrane while fructose requires ATP directly","glucose requires bile-salt micelles while fructose crosses as a free monosaccharide","glucose uses Na+-coupled secondary active transport; fructose uses facilitated diffusion","glucose enters by endocytosis while fructose is co-transported inward with sodium ions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: glucose (and galactose) enter by Na+-dependent SGLT1, whereas fructose enters by facilitated diffusion via GLUT5. — GI Physiology, slide 126"},{"q":"After fatty acids are reassembled into triglycerides inside the enterocyte, they...","choices":["bind intrinsic factor and are carried to the ileum for their later active uptake","are stored in the brush border until bile salts release them to plasma","are packaged into chylomicrons that enter lymph rather than blood capillaries","diffuse directly into portal blood for immediate transport to the liver"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: triglycerides are packaged into chylomicrons, which are too large for capillaries and enter lacteals/lymph, reaching blood via the thoracic duct. — GI Physiology, slides 135-137"},{"q":"Rectal distension triggers defecation through which combined mechanism?","choices":["A hormonal signal from the colon that stimulates mass movements only","A sympathetic reflex that contracts the rectum and relaxes both sphincters","A purely voluntary process controlled entirely by the external sphincter","An intrinsic ENS reflex reinforced by a sacral parasympathetic cord reflex"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: an intrinsic myenteric reflex is strengthened by a sacral (parasympathetic) spinal cord reflex, with voluntary external sphincter control. — GI Physiology, slides 65-67"},{"q":"The liver's role as a blood-glucose buffer means it...","choices":["converts all absorbed glucose immediately into triglyceride for storage","secretes insulin whenever plasma glucose rises above the normal range","prevents any glucose from reaching the systemic circulation directly","stores glucose as glycogen after meals and releases glucose between meals"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the liver takes up and stores glucose as glycogen postprandially and releases glucose during fasting, buffering blood levels. — Ch 71 Liver, slide 9"},{"q":"A key protective function of the liver in protein metabolism is to...","choices":["convert urea back into ammonia so nitrogen can be reused in synthesis","store surplus amino acids as intact protein until the body needs them","excrete surplus amino acids unchanged into the bile","convert ammonia from amino-acid deamination into urea for excretion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the liver converts toxic ammonia (from deamination) into urea via the urea cycle for renal excretion. — Ch 71 Liver, slide 10; Ch 70, slide 16"},{"q":"Under anaerobic conditions, converting pyruvate to lactate is useful mainly because it...","choices":["stores oxygen that the cell can later use inside the mitochondria","produces additional ATP directly from the lactate molecule itself","regenerates NAD+ so that glycolysis can continue producing ATP","removes hydrogen ions that would otherwise raise the cell's pH"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: lactate formation regenerates NAD+, which glycolysis needs to keep running when oxygen is limited. — Ch 68, slides 25-26"},{"q":"Epinephrine and glucagon raise blood glucose partly through phosphorylation, which...","choices":["activates glycogen phosphorylase while inactivating glycogen synthase","activates both glycogen phosphorylase and glycogen synthase together","activates glycogen synthase to build glycogen from incoming glucose","inactivates phosphorylase so stored glycogen is protected from breakdown"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: phosphorylation activates phosphorylase (breakdown) and inactivates synthase (storage), favoring glycogenolysis. — Ch 68, slides 11-12"},{"q":"Which conditions favor hepatic gluconeogenesis?","choices":["A high glucagon-to-insulin ratio with abundant acetyl-CoA from fat oxidation","Depleted acetyl-CoA that halts oxaloacetate formation from pyruvate","Low mitochondrial NADH levels signaling that cellular energy is already plentiful","A high circulating insulin level that drives glucose into muscle and fat cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: gluconeogenesis is favored by a high glucagon:insulin ratio and high mitochondrial acetyl-CoA/NADH from fat oxidation. — Ch 68, slides 30-31"},{"q":"Complete oxidation of one glucose yields far more ATP than glycolysis alone because...","choices":["oxygen is incorporated directly into ATP during the citric acid cycle","the citric acid cycle produces most ATP directly by substrate phosphorylation","NADH and FADH2 feed the electron transport chain to generate most of the ATP","glycolysis is simply repeated several times using the same glucose molecule"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: reduced carriers from glycolysis, pyruvate oxidation, and the citric acid cycle drive oxidative phosphorylation, the main ATP source. — Ch 68, slides 22-23"},{"q":"Which lipoprotein-function pairing is correct?","choices":["VLDL returns excess cholesterol to the liver, while HDL carries the dietary fat","Chylomicrons carry dietary fat from the gut; LDL delivers cholesterol to cells","LDL transports gut triglyceride; chylomicrons deliver hepatic cholesterol","HDL carries dietary triglyceride, while VLDL returns cholesterol back to the gut"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: chylomicrons transport dietary lipid absorbed from the gut, and LDL delivers cholesterol to peripheral cells. — Ch 69, slide 6; GI Physiology, slide 139"},{"q":"During prolonged fasting, the liver produces ketone bodies because...","choices":["abundant circulating glucose drives the conversion of pyruvate directly into ketones","rising insulin stimulates the liver to store its excess fat as ketone bodies","high fatty-acid oxidation generates acetyl-CoA in excess of TCA cycle capacity","amino acids are unavailable, forcing protein to be turned into ketones"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: heavy beta-oxidation floods the liver with acetyl-CoA beyond what the citric acid cycle can handle, so it is diverted to ketones. — Ch 69, slides 15-19"},{"q":"Lipoprotein lipase on the capillary endothelium serves to...","choices":["hydrolyze triglycerides in chylomicrons and VLDL so tissues take up fatty acids","convert cholesterol into bile acids within the hepatocyte for later secretion","assemble chylomicrons from absorbed fatty acids while inside the enterocyte","transport intact triglycerides across the capillary wall into cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: lipoprotein lipase on endothelium hydrolyzes circulating triglyceride, releasing fatty acids for tissue uptake. — Ch 69, slide 13"},{"q":"Which statement about amino-acid metabolism is correct?","choices":["Transamination permanently destroys the carbon skeleton of the amino acid","Transamination builds nonessential amino acids; deamination frees ammonia for urea","Both reactions occur only in muscle tissue and never take place within the liver","Deamination adds an amino group onto a carbon skeleton, a step needing vitamin B6"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: transamination (needing vitamin B6) forms nonessential amino acids, while deamination liberates ammonia that becomes urea. — Ch 70, slides 7-16"},{"q":"Gram for gram, fat provides more than twice the energy of carbohydrate or protein because it...","choices":["requires no oxygen to release its stored chemical energy","yields about 9 kcal/g versus roughly 4 kcal/g for carbohydrate and protein","is absorbed far more completely from the gut than either carbohydrate or protein","is stored together with large amounts of water that add to its measured energy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: fat provides ~9 kcal/g, over double the ~4 kcal/g of carbohydrate or protein. — Ch 72, slide 4"},{"q":"Leptin and ghrelin have opposing effects on appetite because...","choices":["leptin drives hunger during fasting, while ghrelin signals satiety after meals","leptin from the stomach stimulates appetite, while ghrelin suppresses it","leptin from fat reduces appetite, while ghrelin from the stomach stimulates it","both are released from adipose tissue but act on different brain regions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: leptin from adipose tissue signals satiety and reduces intake, while ghrelin from the stomach stimulates hunger. — Ch 72, slides 11-14"},{"q":"Damage to the ventromedial hypothalamic nuclei tends to cause overeating because these nuclei normally...","choices":["release ghrelin that signals the stomach to empty faster","serve as the feeding center that directly drives hunger","detect blood leptin and raise appetite whenever it falls","act as a satiety center that inhibits the feeding center"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the ventromedial nuclei are the satiety center; losing them removes inhibition of feeding, causing hyperphagia. — Ch 72, slide 9"},{"q":"Basal metabolic rate represents...","choices":["the minimal energy needed to sustain the body while at rest and fasting","the energy used only for digesting and absorbing a meal","total daily energy output including all physical activity","the peak energy expenditure that is achievable during heavy physical exercise"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: basal metabolic rate is the minimal energy to maintain the body at rest, measured fasting and thermoneutral. — Ch 73, slides 8, 10"},{"q":"A respiratory quotient near 1.0 shortly after a carbohydrate meal reflects that...","choices":["no gas exchange is occurring at all because metabolism has temporarily paused","protein breakdown dominates the fuel mix, fixing the ratio at exactly 0.8","fat is the main fuel, releasing little CO2 relative to the O2 used","carbohydrate oxidation produces CO2 and consumes O2 in roughly equal amounts"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: burning carbohydrate gives an RQ near 1.0 because CO2 produced and O2 consumed are about equal. — Ch 72, slides 5-6"},{"q":"A protein-rich meal raises metabolic rate more than a fatty meal mainly due to...","choices":["the large thermic effect of processing protein, up to about a 30% rise","protein's higher energy content per gram compared with fat","protein suppressing thyroid hormone and thereby lowering heat loss","protein triggering shivering thermogenesis right after eating"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: protein has the highest thermic effect of food, raising metabolic rate up to ~30%, far more than fat or carbohydrate. — Ch 73, slide 10; Ch 72, slide 9"},{"q":"When surrounding air is cooler than the skin, the body's largest avenue of heat loss at rest is...","choices":["conduction of heat directly into the solid objects being touched","radiation of infrared heat waves to cooler nearby objects","warming of inhaled air within the respiratory passages","evaporation of sweat driven by sympathetic cholinergic fibers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: at rest in cool surroundings, radiation of infrared waves is the dominant route of heat loss. — Ch 74, slide 6"},{"q":"The preoptic area of the anterior hypothalamus regulates temperature by...","choices":["controlling only behavioral responses such as adding or removing clothing","generating body heat directly through its own high metabolic activity","storing a fixed set point that never changes, even during a fever","sensing blood temperature and triggering heat-loss or heat-gain responses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the anterior hypothalamic preoptic area senses core temperature and drives appropriate heat-loss or heat-gain responses around a set point. — Ch 74, slides 5, 16"},{"q":"Sweat reaching the skin surface is normally hypotonic to plasma because...","choices":["potassium fully replaces sodium before the sweat reaches the surface","aldosterone actively adds extra salt into the sweat as it initially forms","the sweat glands secrete pure water that lacks any electrolytes","sodium and chloride are reabsorbed from the duct, especially at low flow"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: as primary secretion moves through the duct, Na+ and Cl- are reabsorbed, leaving hypotonic sweat, most markedly at low flow rates. — Ch 74, slide 8"},{"q":"On exposure to cold, the posterior hypothalamus conserves and generates heat by...","choices":["stimulating sweating and cutaneous vasodilation to trap and hold body warmth","driving cutaneous vasoconstriction and shivering to raise heat production","lowering the overall metabolic rate in order to reduce the body's oxygen demand","inhibiting thyroxine release to slow chemical thermogenesis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: cold responses include sympathetic cutaneous vasoconstriction and shivering, which conserve and generate heat. — Ch 74, slides 14-15"},{"q":"During quiet inspiration, air flows into the alveoli because...","choices":["expiratory muscles compress the chest wall and actively push the air inward","the diaphragm enlarges the thorax, making alveolar pressure sub-atmospheric","surfactant actively pulls air inward across the thin respiratory membrane","the diaphragm relaxes, raising alveolar pressure above atmospheric"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: diaphragmatic contraction expands the thorax, dropping alveolar pressure below atmospheric so air flows in. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 12-14"},{"q":"Airway radius has a powerful effect on airflow because resistance is...","choices":["directly proportional to the radius of the airway","proportional to the square of the airway's length","inversely proportional to the fourth power of the radius","independent of radius and set only by the air's viscosity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: by Poiseuille's relationship, resistance varies with 1/r^4, so small radius changes greatly affect flow. — Pulmonary I & II, slide 16"},{"q":"Lung compliance describes...","choices":["the volume change produced per unit change in distending pressure","the resistance that the airways offer to the flow of moving air","the maximum air volume that the lungs can possibly hold","the tendency of stretched lung tissue to recoil to its resting shape"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: compliance is the change in lung volume per unit change in transmural (distending) pressure. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 14, 19"},{"q":"Surface tension at the air-water interface lining the alveoli tends to...","choices":["hold the alveoli open by pushing their walls apart","increase lung compliance by making expansion easier","draw the alveolar walls together and favor collapse","have no measurable effect on alveolar stability at any size"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: surface tension pulls the liquid-lined walls inward, favoring alveolar collapse unless surfactant opposes it. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 19-20"},{"q":"Surfactant stabilizes small alveoli by...","choices":["increasing the radius of every alveolus throughout the lung","lowering surface tension so small alveoli resist collapsing into larger ones","raising surface tension uniformly across the alveoli of every possible size","thickening the alveolar fluid lining so as to slow the diffusion of gases"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: surfactant reduces surface tension more in small alveoli, preventing them from emptying into larger ones (Laplace). — Pulmonary I & II, slides 22-23"},{"q":"Vital capacity is best described as...","choices":["the air still remaining in the lungs after a maximal forced expiration","the air remaining after a normal quiet expiration","the maximum air expired after a maximal inspiration (IRV + VT + ERV)","the volume of air that is moved during a single normal quiet resting breath"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: vital capacity is the greatest volume that can be expelled after a maximal inspiration, the sum of IRV, tidal volume, and ERV. — Pulmonary I & II, slide 29"},{"q":"Residual volume cannot be measured by simple spirometry because it...","choices":["is the air that remains in the lungs and cannot be exhaled","leaves the lungs completely during a maximal forced expiration","is identical to tidal volume and so needs no measurement","changes with every breath and therefore has no stable value"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: residual volume stays in the lungs even after maximal expiration, so a spirometer cannot capture it directly. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 27, 31"},{"q":"Anatomic dead space reduces the efficiency of ventilation because...","choices":["it is the region where most oxygen uptake actually takes place","air in the conducting airways never reaches alveoli for gas exchange","it holds air that has already fully equilibrated with pulmonary blood","it represents alveoli that are ventilated but not perfused by blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the ~150 mL in conducting airways does not participate in gas exchange, so alveolar ventilation is less than minute ventilation. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 32-35"},{"q":"According to the principles of gas diffusion, transfer across the respiratory membrane increases when...","choices":["the membrane is considerably thicker and the available surface area is smaller","the partial-pressure gradient across the membrane becomes essentially minimal","surface area and the partial-pressure gradient are large and the distance is small","the gas has a very high molecular weight together with a very low solubility"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: diffusion rises with greater area and pressure gradient and shorter diffusion distance, per Fick's principle. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 11, 62"},{"q":"The respiratory membrane is well suited for gas exchange largely because it is...","choices":["richly pigmented so that it can absorb oxygen directly out of the inspired air","thick and muscular so it can withstand high pulmonary pressures","extremely thin (about 0.2 micrometers) with a very large total surface area","lined with beating cilia that actively pump the inspired gases across its surface"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the membrane is only ~0.2 um thick and spans a huge alveolar surface area, ideal for rapid diffusion. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 57-60"},{"q":"Central chemoreceptors in the brainstem adjust ventilation mainly in response to...","choices":["changes in CO2 and H+ within the surrounding brain fluid","changes in arterial oxygen sensed directly within the medulla","blood pressure changes detected at the carotid sinus baroreceptors","stretch signals arriving from receptors located in the lung"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: central chemoreceptors respond primarily to CO2 and H+ in brain extracellular fluid, the strongest minute-to-minute drive. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 91, 96"},{"q":"The carotid and aortic bodies are especially important for detecting...","choices":["the pH of the cerebrospinal fluid around the medulla","a rise in brain interstitial carbon dioxide concentration","stretch of the airway smooth muscle during inspiration","a fall in the arterial partial pressure of oxygen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid and aortic bodies are the main sensors of low arterial oxygen. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 95, 97"},{"q":"Nearly all of the oxygen in blood is carried...","choices":["as bicarbonate formed from the reaction of oxygen and water","dissolved in the plasma, with hemoglobin contributing very little","chemically combined with plasma proteins other than hemoglobin","bound to hemoglobin, with only a small fraction dissolved in plasma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the great majority of oxygen is carried on hemoglobin; only a small amount is physically dissolved. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 76-77"},{"q":"The flat upper portion of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve means that...","choices":["hemoglobin stays highly saturated even when alveolar PO2 falls somewhat","small drops in PO2 cause large drops in saturation within the lungs","oxygen loading depends only on the amount dissolved in plasma","hemoglobin releases all its oxygen the instant PO2 begins to fall"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: on the plateau, saturation remains high despite moderate falls in PO2, providing a loading safety margin in the lungs. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 78-80"},{"q":"A rightward shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve in exercising muscle...","choices":["occurs mainly in the lungs, where it instead speeds pulmonary oxygen loading","prevents any oxygen from being released to the surrounding tissues","increases hemoglobin's oxygen affinity so it holds oxygen more tightly","promotes oxygen unloading where tissue CO2, acid, and temperature are high"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a rightward shift lowers affinity, enhancing oxygen release in active tissues with high CO2, low pH, and heat. — Pulmonary I & II, slide 81"},{"q":"Carbon monoxide impairs oxygen delivery because it...","choices":["destroys the heme iron so that hemoglobin can never re-form","binds hemoglobin far more tightly than oxygen, blocking the O2 sites","dissolves oxygen out of the plasma into the expired air","stimulates excessive oxygen release before blood reaches the tissues"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin with much greater affinity than oxygen, occupying binding sites and reducing O2 carriage. — Pulmonary I & II, slide 82"},{"q":"Most carbon dioxide is transported from the tissues to the lungs...","choices":["bound directly to the heme iron of hemoglobin in place of oxygen molecules","as carbonic acid molecules that circulate freely throughout the plasma","as bicarbonate formed after carbonic anhydrase acts within red cells","dissolved unchanged in the plasma as free carbon dioxide gas"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the majority of CO2 travels as bicarbonate, generated when carbonic anhydrase in red cells hydrates CO2. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 83, 86"},{"q":"The Haldane effect describes how...","choices":["carbon monoxide competitively displaces carbon dioxide out of the plasma","oxygenating hemoglobin in the lungs promotes the release of carbon dioxide","high carbon dioxide levels force hemoglobin to bind still more oxygen molecules","bicarbonate physically prevents hemoglobin from carrying oxygen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the Haldane effect is that binding oxygen reduces hemoglobin's affinity for CO2, aiding CO2 release in the lungs. — Pulmonary I & II, slide 85"},{"q":"A rise in arterial carbon dioxide increases ventilation, which in turn...","choices":["lowers CO2 and raises pH back toward the normal range","raises CO2 even further and drives the pH downward","raises pH by retaining additional carbonic acid in the blood","has no measurable effect on blood pH under any circumstance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: increased ventilation blows off CO2, reducing carbonic acid and returning pH toward normal. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 91-92, 100"},{"q":"At the tissue level, gas exchange occurs because...","choices":["O2 diffuses from blood into cells while CO2 diffuses from cells into blood","both oxygen and carbon dioxide diffuse from the cells into the blood","oxygen moves from cells to blood while carbon dioxide moves from blood to cells","gases move only when they are actively pumped across the capillary wall"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: tissue PO2 is low and PCO2 is high, so oxygen moves into cells and carbon dioxide moves into blood down their gradients. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 72-73"}]};
